</figure>
  </page>
  <page component="ham-not-soln">
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            The graph <m>\bfG_{1}</m> is hamiltonian.
            One example of a hamiltonian cycle is <m>1,5,7,3,2,6,8,9,10,4</m>.
            (There are others.)
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            The graph <m>\bfG_{2}</m> is not hamiltonian.
            One reason for this is that removing the vertex <m>2</m> leaves a disconnected graph.
            Put another way, <m>1,10,4,2</m> forms a cycle, and the only way to get from the other vertices onto that cycle is via vertex <m>2</m>.
            Once you're onto it, there's no way off again.
            Thus, there's no way to construct a cycle containing all <m>10</m> vertices.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            The graph <m>\bfG_{3}</m> is hamiltonian.
            One example of a hamiltonian cycle is <m>1,5,6,2,3,8,9,10,4,7</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            The graph <m>\bfG_{4}</m> is not hamiltonian.
            This is a nontrivial fact.
            For those interested in a complete proof, see the end of this document.
            (You will not be tested on the proof, so it's purely optional reading.)
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            The graph <m>\bfG_{5}</m> (formed by adding one edge to the Petersen graph <m>\bfG_{1}</m>) is hamiltonian.
            One example of a hamiltonian cycle is <m>10,7,1,5,8,4,6,3,2,9</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>


    <proof>
      <title>Proof the Petersen Graph is not hamiltonian</title>

      <p>
        Consider what a hamiltonian cycle in <m>\bfG_{4}</m> would look like.
        The graph has <m>15</m> edges, and a hamiltonian cycle would use <m>10</m> of them.
        Thus, you'd have the cycle and five leftover edges that would connect vertices across the cycle (<q>chords</q>).
        How would these edges fit along the cycle? If an edge connected a vertex to a vertex two steps down the cycle (for example, if the cycle contains the sequence <m>a,b,c</m> of vertices and there's an edge from <m>a</m> to <m>c</m>), then there would be a triangle (a cyle on three vertices) in the graph.
        There is no such cycle in <m>\bfG_{4}</m>.
        If a chord connects a vertex to another vertex at distance three along the hamiltonian cycle, then there is a cycle on four vertices.
        However, there is no such cycle in <m>\bfG_{4}</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Now suppose that a chord connects a vertex <m>v_{0}</m> to another vertex <m>v_{1}</m> at distance four along the hamiltonian cycle.
        Let <m>v_{0}'</m> be the vertex opposite <m>v_{0}</m> along the hamiltonian cycle.
        There is also a chord incident with <m>v_{0}'</m>, connecting it across the hamiltonian cycle to a vertex <m>v_{1}'</m>.
        We've already explained that the distance from <m>v_{0}'</m> to <m>v_{1}'</m> along the hamiltonian cycle cannot be less than four, and it cannot be five since that would give <m>v_{0}'</m> degree four.
        Thus the distance along the hamiltonian cycle from <m>v_{0}'</m> to <m>v_{1}'</m> must be four.
        Now <m>v_{0},v_{1},v_{1}',v_{0}'</m> is a cycle of length four (draw a picture to convince yourself), which we don't have in <m>\bfG_{4}</m>.
        Finally, the only option that remains is that the chords all connect vertices that are exactly opposite each other on the hamiltonian cycle.
        However, then if <m>v_{0}</m> and <m>v_{1}</m> are adjacent along the hamiltonian cycle and <m>v_{0}'</m> and <m>v_{1}'</m> are the vertices opposite them, <m>v_{0},v_{1},v_{1}',v_{0}'</m> is a cycle of length four.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </page>
  </worksheet>
  <worksheet xml:id="ws-triangle-free">
    <title>Graphs with <m>\omega(\bfG)=2</m> and <m>\chi(\bfG)</m> large</title>
    <page>
    <p>
      We've already seen that the chromatic number <m>\chi(\bfG)</m> of a graph <m>\bfG</m> is at least its clique number <m>\omega(\bfG)</m>. (Recall that if <m>\omega(\bfG)=t</m>, then <m>\bfG</m> contains a subgraph isomorphic to <m>\bfK_{t}</m> and <m>\chi(\bfK_{t})=t</m>.) In this activity, we're going to see how to construct graphs with <m>\omega(\bfG)=2</m> and <m>\chi(\bfG)=t</m> for any integer <m>t\geq 2</m>. We will focus only on the construction of Mycielski, which is one of two constructions included in the text.
    </p>
    <p>
      This construction aims to create a graph <m>\bfG_{t}</m> with <m>\chi(\bfG)=t</m> and <m>\omega(\bfG_{t})=2</m>. The construction is inductive. It starts by creating <m>\bfG_{3}</m>, then uses that to make <m>\bfG_{4}</m>, which it uses to make <m>\bfG_{5}</m>, etc. As you go through, discuss the steps with your group members. The questions in <em>italics</em> are questions you must be able to answer to ensure you understand as you go through things.
    </p>
    <exercise workspace="1cm">
      <statement>
        <p>
          We proceed by induction on <m>t</m>. For <m>t=3</m>, we take <m>\bfG_{3}</m> to be the cycle <m>\bfC_{5}</m> on five vertices. (<em>Why is <m>\chi(\bfG_{3})=3</m>? Why is <m>\omega(\bfG_{3})=2</m>?</em>)
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="1.5cm">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Now assume that for some <m>t\ge3</m>, we have determined the graph <m>\bfG_{t}</m>. Suppose that <m>\bfG_{t}</m> has <m>n_{t}</m> vertices. Label the vertices of <m>\bfG_{t}</m> as <m>x_{1},x_{2},\dots,x_{n_t}</m>. Construct <m>\bfG_{t+1}</m> as follows.
        <ol marker="a.">
          <li>
            <p>
              Begin with an independent set <m>I</m> of cardinality <m>n_{t}</m>.
              (<em>What is an independent set?</em>)
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Label the points of <m>I</m> as <m>y_{1},y_{2},\dots,y_{n_t}</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Add a copy of <m>\bfG_{t}</m> with <m>y_{i}</m> adjacent to <m>x_{j}</m> if and only if <m>x_{i}</m> is adjacent to <m>x_{j}</m>.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Attach a new vertex <m>z</m> adjacent to all vertices in <m>I</m> (but none from the copy of <m>\bfG_{t}</m>).
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="1cm">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <em>How many vertices does <m>\bfG_{t+1}</m> contain?</em> (Your formula will contain <m>n_{t}</m>.) You should be able to draw <m>\bfG_{4}</m> by hand, and <m>\bfG_{5}</m> will have <m>23</m> vertices.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  <!-- </page>
  <page> -->
    <exercise>
      <title><m>\omega(\bfG_{t+1})=2</m></title>
      <introduction>

      
<p>
  To see that <m>\omega(\bfG_{t+1})=2</m>, it will suffice to argue that <m>\bfG_{t+1}</m> contains no triangle (<m>\bfK_{3}</m>).
  To do this, we consider where the vertices of a triangle would be:
</p>
</introduction>



  <task workspace="1cm">
    <p>
      Since <m>\bfG_{t}</m> is triangle-free, any triangle in <m>\bfG_{t+1}</m> must contain a vertex of <m>I\cup\{z\}</m>.
    </p>
  </task>

  <task>
    <p>
      Since none of the vertices of <m>I</m> are adjacent (<em>Why?</em>), any triangle in <m>\bfG_{t+1}</m> contains only one vertex of <m>I</m>.
    </p>
  </task>

  <task workspace="1cm">
    <p>
      A triangle cannot contain a vertex of <m>I</m> and the vertex <m>z</m>.
      (<em>Why not?</em> Consider where the third vertex is.)
    </p>
  </task>

  <task>
    <p>
      A triangle must thus contain a vertex <m>y_{i}\in I</m> and vertices <m>x_{j}</m> and <m>x_{k}</m> from the copy of <m>\bfG_{t}</m>.
    </p>
  </task>

  <task workspace="1cm">
    <p>
      <em>Why does <m>\{y_{i},x_{j},x_{k}\}</m> forming a triangle mean that there's a triangle in <m>\bfG_{t}</m>?</em>
    </p>
  </task>

</exercise>
</page>
<page>
<exercise>
  <title><m>\chi(\bfG_{t+1})=t+1</m></title>
  <task workspace="1cm">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <em>What part of the construction ensures that <m>\chi(\bfG_{t+1})\ge t</m>?</em>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
  <task workspace="2cm">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <em>How can you color the vertices of <m>I</m> using <m>t</m> colors?</em> (Hint: Pair vertices of <m>I</m> with vertices of <m>\bfG_{t}</m>.) <em>What color does <m>z</m> get? How many colors have you used and what does this say about <m>\chi(\bfG_{t+1})</m>?</em>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
  <task workspace="2cm">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Try coloring <m>\bfG_{t+1}</m> using only <m>t</m> colors. Suppose that <m>\phi</m> is a proper coloring of <m>\bfG_{t+1}</m> using the colors <m>\{1,2,\dots,t\}</m>. It's safe to assume that <m>\phi(z) = t</m> (reshuffle the colors otherwise).
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
  <task workspace="2cm">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>S</m> be the set of vertices in the copy of <m>\bfG_{t}</m> to which <m>\phi</m> assigns color <m>t</m>.
<em>Can the set <m>S</m> be empty? Explain your reasoning.</em>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
  <task workspace="1.5cm">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Since <m>\phi(z)=t</m>, <em>what do you know about the set (or even number) of colors used on <m>I</m>?</em>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
  <task workspace="1.5cm">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <em>Why can you change the color of each <m>x_{i}\in S</m> to match the color <m>\phi</m> assigns to <m>y_{i}</m> and maintain a proper coloring?</em>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
  <task workspace="1cm">
    <statement>
      <p>
        <em>Now how many colors have you used on the copy of <m>\bfG_{t}</m>?</em>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </task>
</exercise>
</page>
  </worksheet>
</chapter>